.TH AENIGMA-LAUNCHER 8 "2026-08-13" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-launcher \- launch the Aenigma application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-launcher
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-launcher
starts the Aenigma application binary,
.IR /usr/lib/aenigma/Enigma5.App ,
pointing it at the standard configuration file,
.IR /etc/aenigma/appsettings.json .
It takes no options or arguments of its own.
.PP
This script is intended to be invoked by the
.B aenigma.service
systemd unit as the service's entry point, rather than run
interactively, though it may also be run directly for testing or
troubleshooting.
.PP
The script does not fork, daemonize, or redirect output; it runs
.B Enigma5.App
in the foreground and exits with whatever status
.B Enigma5.App
itself returns.
.SH OPTIONS
.B aenigma-launcher
takes no command-line options. All application configuration is
read by
.B Enigma5.App
itself from
.IR /etc/aenigma/appsettings.json ,
which can be modified using
.BR aenigma-config (8).
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status is whatever
.B /usr/lib/aenigma/Enigma5.App
returns. A non-zero status may also result if the binary or the
configuration file referenced on its command line cannot be found.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/aenigma/Enigma5.App
The Aenigma application binary, invoked by this script.
.TP
.I /etc/aenigma/appsettings.json
The application configuration file, passed to
.B Enigma5.App
via its
.B \-\-config
option.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the Aenigma application directly, in the foreground:
.PP
.RS
.EX
aenigma-launcher
.EE
.RE
.PP
Under systemd, the service is normally started via
.BR aenigma-start (8)
rather than by invoking this script directly.
.SH NOTES
Unlike the other Aenigma administration scripts, this script does
.B not
require and does not check for root privileges; it runs
.B Enigma5.App
as whatever user invokes it. When started via
.BR systemd (1),
the
.B aenigma.service
unit is expected to specify
.B User=aenigma
(or equivalent) so that the application runs as the unprivileged
.B aenigma
service account rather than as root; this script itself performs no
privilege check or drop.
.PP
Neither the application binary path nor the configuration file path
is verified to exist before executing; if either is missing, the
resulting failure is whatever error
.B Enigma5.App
(or the shell, if the binary itself is absent) produces, rather than
a descriptive error from this script.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aenigma-start (8),
.BR aenigma-config (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
